  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B033",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B024",
    "B014"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged: B033 remains the most complete present case. The magic-wavelength lattice-clock idea and its decisive experimental development have yielded an independently reproduced regime of precision measurement, with established reach into gravity, relativity, and tests of fundamental physics. This is not a prize for a prospective standards decision; it is for a mature physical invention that has made new clues from nature accessible. On the evidence before us, Katori and Ye still give the most compact and intelligible attribution from originating concept to defining development.",
    "case_against": "The principal reservation remains historical rather than scientific. Optical lattice clocks stand within a longer lineage of optical-frequency metrology, laser control, and atomic-clock work, and a final archival review must establish that no indispensable contribution is omitted by the proposed pair. The citation must also delimit the lattice-clock invention and development rather than imply credit for the entire history of optical clocks or for a future redefinition of the second.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "pearce-mark",
        "point": "Pearce maintains that B006 opened a distinct, mature ultrafast X-ray observational capability, but identifies the distributed conceptual, accelerator, facility, and technical record as its central attribution difficulty.",
        "response": "I agree that B006 represents a genuinely powerful new way of observing matter and that its multiple realizations establish maturity. But the attribution concern is decisive for this decision: the facility-scale achievement cannot be cleanly separated from a broad chain of indispensable work using only the proposed trio. B033 is also experimentally mature while preserving a more coherent link between the central physical idea, its development, and the credited names."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig argues that B008 has exceptional universal and explanatory permanence, while accepting that its antecedents, the distinct roles of the proposed trio, and the boundary of the cited achievement require scrutiny.",
        "response": "I respect the case for B008, especially its long-lived bridge between critical phenomena and quantum field theory. Yet its theoretical elegance cannot by itself settle a prize whose exact historical boundary and attribution remain open. My preference for B033 reflects the additional strength of a tightly defined physical innovation whose extraordinary performance is secure across laboratories and directly supports further tests of nature."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson judges B014 to be experimentally secure and far-reaching but stresses that the Aharonov-Bohm effect and Berry phase have separate historical strands, making a single citation delicate.",
        "response": "That is why B014 remains one of my alternatives rather than part of the preferred configuration. Its conceptual consequences are profound, but joining the two discoveries could obscure rather than clarify their separate origins. B033 has the cleaner single-achievement narrative, provided the requested review confirms that Katori and Ye capture the indispensable lattice-clock work."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether archival review confirms that Katori and Ye alone capture the indispensable invention and early development of optical lattice clocks.",
      "How narrowly the final citation can distinguish optical lattice clocks from the wider history of optical atomic clocks and from any future SI decision."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
